# Responsive Web Design — Overview

## 1. Responsive Web Design

Responsive web design makes a website adapt to different screen sizes, including desktops, laptops, tablets and phones.

## 2. Media Queries

Media queries apply CSS rules when specified conditions are met, such as viewport width.

```css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .container {
    width: 100%;
  }
}
```

Use them to adjust layouts and styles for different viewport sizes.

## 3. Flexbox

Flexbox is a one-dimensional CSS layout system. It arranges items in a row or column.

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  gap: 16px;
}
```

Useful for navigation bars, aligned components and flexible rows or columns.

## 4. CSS Grid

CSS Grid is a two-dimensional layout system that arranges items in rows and columns.

```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  gap: 16px;
}
```

Useful for card layouts, galleries and structured page sections.

## 5. Bootstrap

Bootstrap is a front-end framework providing CSS utilities, responsive grid classes and prebuilt components.

It helps developers build responsive interfaces without writing every style from scratch.

## Key Takeaways

- **Responsive design:** The overall approach to adapting interfaces to different screens.
- **Media queries:** Apply styles based on conditions.
- **Flexbox:** Arrange items along one dimension.
- **CSS Grid:** Arrange items across rows and columns.
- **Bootstrap:** A framework with reusable styling tools and components.

These tools can be used together. Flexbox and Grid handle layouts, media queries adjust styles when needed, and Bootstrap provides an existing toolkit for building interfaces.
